WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.550
- I say put mental health first,

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:06.210
because if you don't, then
you're not gonna enjoy your sport

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:08.460
and you're not gonna succeed
as much as you want to.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.260
So it's okay sometimes to even sit out

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.810
of the big competitions
to focus on yourself,

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:15.460
because it shows how
strong of a competitor

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.710
and person that you really are

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.094
rather than just battle through it.

00:00:20.094 --> 00:00:22.677
(upbeat music)

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.670
- [Jason] We've seen
increasingly that athletes

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:32.330
are getting comfortable with the idea

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:34.260
of discussing mental health

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.890
and the idea of treating
their state of mind,

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:41.230
and mental health as kind of a body part

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:43.580
in the same way they would
attend to a sprained ankle

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:44.760
or a pulled hamstring

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.780
that they are gonna take time to recover

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.340
if they're feeling any
kind of mental duress.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:50.670
And I think it's a really healthy

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:53.110
and productive conversation to have.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:55.890
Teams and franchises
and athletes and coaches

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.010
now treat physical injury different

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.810
than they did a generation ago.

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:02.560
The idea of like, just icing somebody down

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.730
or like slapping them
upside the head and saying,

00:01:04.730 --> 00:01:07.180
get back out there and do it for the team

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.140
is increasingly a fading notion.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:15.440
And mental health is sort of
a new part of the prism here.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.450
The idea that teams
would have an investment

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:19.720
and not just the mental health

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:23.140
that applies to their
execution on the playing field,

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.470
but just their general health
and happiness is a new thing.

00:01:27.470 --> 00:01:30.390
And I think that it actually in this case

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.820
an interesting way has
been athlete driven.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.720
It's the athletes who are coming forward

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.830
and talking with great candor
about their own struggles.

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:43.180
- And I think we're just a
little bit too stressed out,

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.740
but we should be out here having fun

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.460
and sometimes that's not the case.

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.070
- In the past we know that

00:01:49.070 --> 00:01:51.390
people have not talked
about mental health,

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.900
especially in the public realm

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.390
because there's so much
stigma that exists.

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:58.240
And while that stigma still exists,

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:00.880
we see a lot of high profile athletes

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:02.940
talking about their mental health.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:04.130
On the clinical side,

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:08.270
we also see a lot more cooperation
with larger organizations

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:09.860
to try and understand

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.670
what it means to have a
mental health diagnosis

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:16.090
and what that might look like
for their individual athletes.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:19.260
A lot of people think
that athletes just appear

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:23.100
and show us their natural
grace and abilities.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:23.933
And while it's true,

00:02:23.933 --> 00:02:26.900
many of them have outstanding
physical abilities

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.050
and we love watching them perform,

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:30.830
it takes many, many hours

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.370
to get them in that
place of mental toughness

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:36.730
to be able to execute
their skills on demand.

00:02:36.730 --> 00:02:39.220
So I think that it's important
for people to understand

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.290
that even though they see
that public excellence,

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.270
sometimes there's quite
a bit of private anguish.

00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:48.140
It's important for people to realize that

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:52.160
these athletes are humans
first, they have emotions,

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.140
they have lives outside of sport.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:57.680
So it's important for them to
take care of themselves too.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.120
- [Jason] We've already seen

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.790
in the conversation that's happening

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:05.090
and the elevation of the
topic, not just in sports,

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:07.820
but in, workplaces at large,

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.730
a real benefit for people to feel

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:13.330
that they can have the
liberty and the courage

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:16.440
to come forward and have
these kinds of conversations.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.580
That kind of comfort, I think
is a real important thing.

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:22.250
And we've seen it in the past

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:24.900
that sports can often be a vessel

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.920
for broader societal conversations

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.755
and I think that's what's happening here.

00:03:28.755 --> 00:03:31.338
(upbeat music)

